# quillen_detecting_subgroup.g — Q-9 / Issue #9 / Task #61.
#
# Detecting-subgroup variant for H^3(E_+(m); U(1)).
#
# # Background.
#
# An independent path to H^3(G; U(1)) for a finite p-group G uses a
# detecting subgroup H <= G — a subgroup such that the restriction
#
#     res^G_H : H^*(G; U(1)) -> H^*(H; U(1))
#
# is INJECTIVE (Adem-Milgram [AM04, §V.6]). Then H^*(G; U(1)) is
# recovered as a sub-Z/exp module of H^*(H; U(1)) under res.
#
# For an extraspecial 2-group E_+(m) (= D_8^{*m} central product),
# [AM04, §IV.4-§IV.5 (Thm 4.3, Thm 5.6)] gives a detecting family
# consisting of:
#   * the FULL set of N_+(m, 2) = prod_{i=0..m-1}(2^i + 1) maximal
#     elementary abelian subgroups of rank m+1 (= 6, 30, 270, 4590,
#     75582, 9845550 at m = 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7). One representative
#     per Aut(G)-orbit does NOT suffice — see the erratum below; and
#   * one D_8 = E_+(1) section (any "fundamental" rank-2 nonabelian
#     section, e.g. generated by z, σ_{1,1}, σ_{1,2} — this is E_+(1)).
#
# # Erratum (per #289 audit, corrected in #291).
#
# An earlier draft of this header (and of methodology paper §3.2 +
# R-1 lit survey Step 3) claimed "the m+1 conjugates of a maximal
# elementary abelian" suffice. That was wrong: the kernel of
# restriction to a single maximal elementary abelian is generally
# non-trivial. Aut(G) is transitive on type-+ Lagrangians for m >= 2
# (single Aut-orbit at the subgroup level), but Aut-equivariance
# acts at the cocycle-mod-coboundary level, not the subgroup level —
# Aut(G) sends ker(res_{EA_1}) to ker(res_{EA_2}), not back to
# itself. Empirically refuted at m=2 (generators 1 and 5 of
# H^3(E_+(2); U(1)) have zero restriction to a single max EA but
# are detected by other max EAs). The D-VAN m=5 fix (#287) uses
# a 112-Lagrangian subset (32 axis-aligned + 80 diagonal pair-swap)
# that is empirically sufficient (0/165 false trivials observed)
# but theoretically under-justified vs the full 4590-Lagrangian
# family Quillen's theorem requires. See the audit research note
# at `docs/superpowers/research/2026-05-19-d-van-detecting-family-
# correctness.md` for the full reconciliation.
# The citation chain in detail: Adem-Milgram framework (§IV.4-§IV.5)
# -> Quillen 1971 Thm 5.10 (explicit H*(E_+(m); F_2)) -> Harada-Kono
# 1987 Thm 1.1 (integer A(k) summand) -> UCT for U(1) coefficients.
# See R-1 (docs/superpowers/research/2026-05-17-detecting-family-literature.md)
# for the full synthesis.
#
# The union F = E ∪ D, where E is the maximal elementary abelian
# rank-(m+1) and D = E_+(1) is the D_8 section, is a *detecting family*
# in the AM04 sense, but for a pragmatic single-subgroup
# `UnitCircleCohomology` API we use the WEAKER form: a single subgroup
# H of "mostly-detecting" character. The single maximal elementary
# abelian E detects all 2-torsion of H^3(G; U(1)) — the most
# numerically demanding part — but it does NOT detect higher
# (4-, 8-)torsion. For E_+(2), this is fine for 9 of the 10 invariant
# factors; the order-8 piece must come from elsewhere (e.g. from the
# D_8 section, where Bockstein gives Z/8).
#
# The user-facing acceptance criteria (issue #9) are:
#   * m = 2, 3: agrees with direct HAP `GroupCohomology(G, 3)`
#   * m = 7: produces torsion vector (HAP-tractable via detecting
#     subgroup even when direct HAP on G is not).
#
# In this file we implement:
#
#   * MaximalElementaryAbelian_E_plus(m)
#       Build the maximal elementary abelian E ≤ E_+(m) of rank m+1
#       as a subgroup of E_+(m) (the explicit matrix realisation in
#       em_construction.g). The generators are the centre z together
#       with σ_{a,1} for a = 1..m. Returns a record with the subgroup
#       and the parent group.
#
#   * D8Section_E_plus(m)
#       The D_8 = E_+(1) section <z, σ_{1,1}, σ_{1,2}> ≤ E_+(m).
#       Returns the subgroup and the parent group.
#
#   * H3DetectingSubgroup(G, m)
#       Compute H^3(H; U(1)) for the detecting subgroup H of E_+(m)
#       using HAP. Returns rec(.torsion, .group_id, .subgroup_id, ...).
#       The "detecting" subgroup is the maximal elementary abelian.
#
#   * UnitCircleCohomologyDetecting(G, n, m)
#       Alternative `UnitCircleCohomology`-style API. Returns a record
#       with .torsion the H^n torsion vector of the detecting
#       subgroup. For n = 3 and m in {2, 3} this is an UPPER BOUND on
#       the torsion of H^3(G; U(1)) (with the caveat above about
#       higher-torsion components).
#
# Convention. H^3(G; U(1)) ≅ H^3(G; Z) for finite G via the standard
# Bockstein in the SES 0 -> Z -> R -> U(1) -> 0 with R cohomologically
# trivial. Throughout we use HAP's `GroupCohomology(H, 3)` which returns
# the torsion of H^3(H; Z). The U(1) statement is identical at the
# level of torsion vectors.
#
# # Dependencies.
#   * HAP package (loaded via LoadPackage("hap")).
#   * em_construction.g — explicit matrix realisation of E_+(m) (used
#     for the maximal-elementary-abelian construction). Loaded
#     lazily.
#
# # Usage.
#
#     ChangeDirectoryCurrent("/abs/path/to/extraspecial_orbifolds/scripts");
#     Read("quillen_detecting_subgroup.g");
#     rec := UnitCircleCohomologyDetecting(2);   # m = 2
#     # rec.torsion is the H^3 torsion vector of the detecting subgroup
#     # (Z/2)^3 ≤ E_+(2).
#
# # Invariant.
#   * For all m >= 1, the detecting subgroup has order 2^{m+1} (rank
#     m+1 elementary abelian) and is HAP-tractable.

# ---- HAP ---------------------------------------------------------------

LoadPackage("hap");

# ---- modular_data.g (lazy) for UniversalCoefficientsTheorem ------------
# We need UCT for the H^3(G; U(1)) torsion convention used throughout
# this repo. The function lives in DRINFELD-style src/modular_data.g.
# Use INPUT_FILENAME() to locate it robustly, then fall back to
# CWD-relative candidates.

_qd_uct_loaded := IsBoundGlobal("UniversalCoefficientsTheorem");;
if not _qd_uct_loaded then
    _qd_uct_self := INPUT_FILENAME();;
    if _qd_uct_self <> fail and _qd_uct_self <> "stream" then
        _qd_uct_dir := _qd_uct_self{[1..Length(_qd_uct_self) -
                          Length("/quillen_detecting_subgroup.g")]};;
        for _qd_p in [ "/../../src/modular_data.g",
                       "/../../../src/modular_data.g",
                       "/../../DRINFELD/src/modular_data.g" ] do
            if not _qd_uct_loaded then
                _qd_uct_cand := Concatenation(_qd_uct_dir, _qd_p);;
                if IsExistingFile(_qd_uct_cand) then
                    Read(_qd_uct_cand);
                    _qd_uct_loaded := true;
                fi;
            fi;
        od;
    fi;
    if not _qd_uct_loaded then
        for _qd_p in [ "../../src/modular_data.g",
                       "../../../src/modular_data.g",
                       "src/modular_data.g",
                       "DRINFELD/src/modular_data.g" ] do
            if not _qd_uct_loaded and IsExistingFile(_qd_p) then
                Read(_qd_p);
                _qd_uct_loaded := true;
            fi;
        od;
    fi;
    if not _qd_uct_loaded then
        Print("WARNING: could not locate src/modular_data.g; ",
              "UniversalCoefficientsTheorem not available. ",
              "H3UnitCircleViaUCT and the anchor test will fail.\n");
    fi;
fi;

# ---- Locate em_construction.g lazily -----------------------------------
#
# Pattern matches `quillen_presentation.g`: use INPUT_FILENAME() to
# locate the sibling file robustly across CWDs.

_qd_em_loaded := false;;
_qd_self_path := INPUT_FILENAME();;
if _qd_self_path <> fail and _qd_self_path <> "stream" then
    _qd_self_dir := _qd_self_path{[1..Length(_qd_self_path) -
                                      Length("/quillen_detecting_subgroup.g")]};;
    _qd_candidate := Concatenation(_qd_self_dir, "/em_construction.g");;
    if IsExistingFile(_qd_candidate) then
        Read(_qd_candidate);
        _qd_em_loaded := true;
    fi;
fi;
if not _qd_em_loaded then
    for _qd_p in [ "em_construction.g",
                   "extraspecial_orbifolds/scripts/em_construction.g",
                   "scripts/em_construction.g" ] do
        if not _qd_em_loaded and IsExistingFile(_qd_p) then
            Read(_qd_p);
            _qd_em_loaded := true;
        fi;
    od;
fi;
# em_construction.g is optional — only needed for the explicit matrix
# realisation. We can also work directly with ExtraspecialGroup(...).

# ---- E_+(m) construction (matches identify_extraspecial.g) ------------
#
# Use GAP's built-in ExtraspecialGroup(2^{2m+1}, "+") for arbitrary m.
# For m in {1, 2, 3} this also has known SmallGroup IDs:
#     E_+(1) = SmallGroup(8, 3)     = D_8
#     E_+(2) = SmallGroup(32, 49)
#     E_+(3) = SmallGroup(128, 2326)
# For m = 4, 5, 6, 7, the group has |G| > 256 and falls outside the
# SmallGroup library — we use ExtraspecialGroup directly.

EPlusGroup := function(m)
    return ExtraspecialGroup(2^(2*m + 1), "+");
end;;

# ---- Maximal elementary abelian subgroup of E_+(m) --------------------
#
# In E_+(m) = <z, σ_{a,1}, σ_{a,2} : a in 1..m> with
#   z central, z^2 = 1
#   σ_{a,r}^2 = 1
#   [σ_{a,1}, σ_{a,2}] = z
#   [σ_{a,r}, σ_{b,s}] = 1 for a != b
# the m+1 elements { z, σ_{1,1}, σ_{2,1}, ..., σ_{m,1} } all commute
# and are each of order 2 (the σ_{a,1} are pairwise commuting; z is
# central). Their subgroup is elementary abelian of rank m+1, which is
# maximal (any further element would be a σ_{a,2} which anti-commutes
# with σ_{a,1}).
#
# Working through GAP's ExtraspecialGroup PC presentation: the
# generators are returned in a fixed order. We use the SECOND HALF of
# the convention "pair up adjacent generators": for m = 1,
# ExtraspecialGroup(8, "+") = <g1, g2, g3> with g3 = [g1, g2] central
# of order 2. For m > 1, the structure is the central product, with
# the centre identified, so the generator list has 2m + 1 elements
# in the order (a1, b1, a2, b2, ..., am, bm, z) where z is central.
# We pick z together with a1, a2, ..., am.
#
# Implementation choice. Rather than rely on GAP's PC generator
# convention (which is fragile across versions), we identify the
# centre and "decompose" the group as follows:
#   1. Find the centre Z(G) — it is cyclic of order 2 for an
#      extraspecial group. Its non-trivial element is z.
#   2. Find a complement of Z in G/[G,G]: i.e. lift representatives
#      of the elementary abelian quotient G/Z back to G. Pick a set
#      of m commuting representatives that are independent mod Z.
#
# This is what `MaximalElementaryAbelian_E_plus` does. It uses GAP's
# `MaximalElementaryAbelianSubgroups` (returning a list of conjugacy
# class reps) — every extraspecial 2-group of plus type has a maximal
# elementary abelian of rank m + 1.

MaximalElementaryAbelian_E_plus := function(m)
    local G, expected_rank, expected_order, all_reps, abelians,
          candidates, H;
    G := EPlusGroup(m);
    expected_rank := m + 1;
    expected_order := 2^(m + 1);

    # GAP / HAP do not expose a `MaximalElementaryAbelianSubgroups`
    # function in stock GAP 4.15. We use the explicit construction
    # via subgroup-class enumeration.
    #
    # For an extraspecial 2-group of plus type, every maximal
    # elementary abelian has rank m + 1 (= one more than half the
    # rank of G/Z). They form several conjugacy classes (6 for
    # E_+(2), more for larger m), but all are isomorphic; pick the
    # first.
    #
    # Enumeration via ConjugacyClassesSubgroups is O(class count)
    # which is tractable up to |G| = 2^15 (E_+(7), 32768) but
    # potentially slow. For m >= 4 we'd prefer a direct construction
    # (centre + commuting σ_{a,1}'s).
    #
    # Construction shortcut for arbitrary m: use the central product
    # decomposition. The HAP `ExtraspecialGroup(2^{2m+1}, '+')`
    # construction returns a PC group; we extract the centre, then
    # for each of m commuting "pair generators" find a single
    # representative.
    #
    # For initial implementation, enumerate. For m = 7, fall back to
    # direct construction (see below).

    if m <= 3 then
        # Enumeration path: feasible for E_+(1), E_+(2), E_+(3).
        all_reps := List(ConjugacyClassesSubgroups(G),
                         C -> Representative(C));
        abelians := Filtered(all_reps,
                             S -> IsElementaryAbelian(S));
        candidates := Filtered(abelians,
                               S -> Order(S) = expected_order);
        if Length(candidates) = 0 then
            Error("MaximalElementaryAbelian_E_plus: no rank-",
                  expected_rank, " elementary abelian in E_+(", m, ")");
        fi;
        H := candidates[1];
    else
        # Direct construction for large m: build elementary abelian
        # from the centre + a set of m commuting non-central
        # generators. See `BuildElementaryAbelianDirect_E_plus`.
        H := BuildElementaryAbelianDirect_E_plus(G, m);
    fi;

    return rec( parent := G,
                subgroup := H,
                rank := expected_rank,
                m := m,
                order := Order(H) );
end;;

# ---- Direct elementary abelian construction (for large m) -------------
#
# For m >= 4, ConjugacyClassesSubgroups becomes prohibitively expensive.
# We instead build the maximal elementary abelian directly using the
# group's structure as an extraspecial group:
#
#   1. The centre Z = <z> of G is cyclic of order 2.
#   2. The quotient G/Z is elementary abelian of rank 2m, and the
#      commutator map G/Z × G/Z -> Z is a non-degenerate symplectic
#      form (over F_2) when m >= 1 with type '+'.
#   3. A Lagrangian (maximal isotropic) subspace L ≤ G/Z of dimension
#      m lifts to a maximal elementary abelian L_lift ≤ G of rank m+1
#      (preimage of L under G -> G/Z).
#
# # Closed-form path (audit C1, issue #458).
#
# When G is GAP's stock `ExtraspecialGroup(2^(2m+1), "+")`, the PC
# presentation orders generators as [a_1, b_1, a_2, b_2, ..., a_m, b_m, z]
# with `[a_i, b_i] = z` and all other commutators trivial. So
# {a_1, a_2, ..., a_m, z} = {gens[1], gens[3], ..., gens[2m-1], z}
# is always a maximal elementary abelian of rank m+1.
#
# We detect this convention by checking the generator count (2m+1)
# AND the commutator pattern (odd-indexed gens pairwise commute, each
# odd-indexed gen has order 2). If both hold we return the closed-form
# subgroup; otherwise we fall through to the legacy greedy search.
#
# Note: m=1 is special — `ExtraspecialGroup(8, "+") = D_8` has
# `gens[1]^2 = z` (gens[1] is the order-4 rotation), so the
# closed-form is m >= 2 only.
#
# Legacy algorithm (fallback):
#   * Get a generating set of G (modulo Z).
#   * Greedy-add commuting non-central generators.
#   * If greedy fails, brute-force Combinations([1..2m], m).
#   * Lift representatives to G.
#   * Form the subgroup generated by z and the m lifted generators.

# Closed-form helper: try the PC convention. Returns the EA subgroup, or
# `fail` if the PC convention is not recognised.
_TryPCClosedFormEA_EPlus := function(G, m)
    local gens, n, z, odd_gens, i, j, H;
    if m < 2 then
        # m=1 has gens[1] of order 4 (D_8 rotation): closed-form does
        # not apply.
        return fail;
    fi;
    gens := GeneratorsOfGroup(G);
    n := Length(gens);
    # Expected: |gens| = 2m + 1 with gens[2m+1] = z.
    if n <> 2 * m + 1 then
        return fail;
    fi;
    # Last generator should be the centre (order 2, central).
    z := gens[n];
    if not IsOne(z^2) or Order(z) <> 2 then
        return fail;
    fi;
    if not (z in Centre(G)) then
        return fail;
    fi;
    # Sanity-check: gens[1], gens[3], ..., gens[2m-1] are non-central,
    # order 2, pairwise commute.
    odd_gens := List([1, 3 .. 2 * m - 1], i -> gens[i]);
    for i in [1..m] do
        if Order(odd_gens[i]) <> 2 then return fail; fi;
        if odd_gens[i] in Centre(G) then return fail; fi;
    od;
    for i in [1..m] do
        for j in [i + 1 .. m] do
            if Comm(odd_gens[i], odd_gens[j]) <> One(G) then
                return fail;
            fi;
        od;
    od;
    # Construct the rank-(m+1) elementary abelian.
    H := Subgroup(G, Concatenation(odd_gens, [z]));
    if Order(H) <> 2^(m + 1) or not IsElementaryAbelian(H) then
        # Defensive: PC convention looks right but subgroup is wrong.
        # Bail out and let the greedy fallback handle it.
        return fail;
    fi;
    return H;
end;;

# Legacy greedy + brute-force search. Retained as fallback for groups
# whose PC presentation does not match the canonical convention (e.g.
# isomorphic copies built via FreeGroup quotients).
_LegacyGreedyEA_EPlus := function(G, m)
    local Z_G, z, basis_lifts, h, i, idx, subgens, H, gens_G;

    Z_G := Centre(G);
    z := First(AsList(Z_G), x -> not IsOne(x));

    # Generators of G that are not in Z(G): we want a set of 2m
    # elements that map to a basis of G/Z (which has dimension 2m).
    # Use GeneratorsOfGroup; for the GAP ExtraspecialGroup, this
    # already gives such a set.
    gens_G := Filtered(GeneratorsOfGroup(G), g -> not (g in Z_G));
    if Length(gens_G) < 2*m then
        # Ensure we have enough generators: extend by random elements
        # if needed (shouldn't happen for canonical extraspecials).
        for h in AsList(G) do
            if not (h in Z_G) and Length(gens_G) < 2*m then
                Add(gens_G, h);
            fi;
        od;
    fi;
    # Truncate.
    gens_G := gens_G{[1..2*m]};

    # Compute commutator pattern: [gens[i], gens[j]] is either 1 or z.
    # We want to find m elements gens[i] that pairwise commute.
    # Use a greedy approach: pick the first generator, then iteratively
    # add generators that commute with all already-picked ones.
    basis_lifts := [];
    for i in [1..2*m] do
        h := gens_G[i];
        # Does h commute with everything in basis_lifts?
        if ForAll(basis_lifts, b -> Comm(h, b) = One(G)) then
            Add(basis_lifts, h);
            if Length(basis_lifts) >= m then break; fi;
        fi;
    od;

    # If we didn't find m commuting generators (the order-of-traversal
    # was unlucky), fall back to a more careful search: explicitly
    # search through products of generators.
    if Length(basis_lifts) < m then
        basis_lifts := [];
        # Try: for each subset of m elements among gens_G, check
        # they pairwise commute and are independent mod Z.
        # For 2m up to 14 (m=7) this is at most C(14, 7) = 3432
        # subsets, still tractable.
        for idx in Combinations([1..2*m], m) do
            subgens := List(idx, i -> gens_G[i]);
            if ForAll([1..m], i ->
                      ForAll([1..m], j ->
                             Comm(subgens[i], subgens[j]) = One(G))) then
                # Independent mod Z: check no product of subgens is in Z.
                # Sufficient: subgens are distinct mod Z (each non-central,
                # and no two equal mod Z). Quick check via subgroup order.
                H := Subgroup(G, Concatenation([z], subgens));
                if Order(H) = 2^(m+1) and IsElementaryAbelian(H) then
                    basis_lifts := subgens;
                    break;
                fi;
            fi;
        od;
    fi;

    if Length(basis_lifts) < m then
        Error("BuildElementaryAbelianDirect_E_plus: could not find ",
              m, " commuting independent non-central generators");
    fi;

    H := Subgroup(G, Concatenation([z], basis_lifts));
    if Order(H) <> 2^(m+1) or not IsElementaryAbelian(H) then
        Error("BuildElementaryAbelianDirect_E_plus: constructed subgroup ",
              "has order ", Order(H), ", expected ", 2^(m+1));
    fi;
    return H;
end;;

BuildElementaryAbelianDirect_E_plus := function(G, m)
    local H_cf;
    # Closed-form path (audit C1): canonical PC presentation of
    # ExtraspecialGroup(2^(2m+1), "+") gives a maximal EA directly.
    H_cf := _TryPCClosedFormEA_EPlus(G, m);
    if H_cf <> fail then
        return H_cf;
    fi;
    # Fall through to the legacy greedy + brute-force search.
    return _LegacyGreedyEA_EPlus(G, m);
end;;

# ---- D_8 = E_+(1) section ---------------------------------------------
#
# A D_8 section of E_+(m) is generated by the centre z together with
# σ_{1,1} and σ_{1,2} — i.e. the "first factor" of the central product
# E_+(m) = D_8 *_Z D_8 *_Z ... *_Z D_8. Pragmatically, we pick any
# subgroup isomorphic to D_8 inside E_+(m). For m = 1, this is G itself.

D8Section_E_plus := function(m)
    local G, maxsub, candidates, H, gens_G, H_small;
    G := EPlusGroup(m);
    if m = 1 then
        return rec( parent := G,
                    subgroup := G,
                    m := m,
                    order := Order(G),
                    note := "for m=1 the D_8 section is G itself" );
    fi;
    # For m <= 3, the full enumeration via ConjugacyClassesSubgroups is
    # fast (|G| <= 128). For m >= 4 (|G| >= 512), the enumeration becomes
    # prohibitive; the standard E_+(m) PC presentation already exposes a
    # canonical D_8 = E_+(1) as the subgroup generated by the first two
    # non-central generators (per the (a_1, b_1, a_2, b_2, ..., z)
    # convention from the header). We use that directly.
    if m >= 4 then
        gens_G := GeneratorsOfGroup(G);
        # First two generators are a_1 and b_1; their subgroup is D_8.
        H := Subgroup(G, [gens_G[1], gens_G[2]]);
        if Order(H) <> 8 or IdGroup(H) <> [8, 3] then
            Error("D8Section_E_plus: direct construction at m = ", m,
                  " did not yield D_8 (order ", Order(H), ", id ",
                  IdGroup(H), "). PC-generator convention has changed?");
        fi;
        return rec( parent := G,
                    subgroup := H,
                    m := m,
                    order := Order(H),
                    note := "D_8 = E_+(1) section (direct, m >= 4)" );
    fi;
    # m in {2, 3}: enumeration path.
    # Find a D_8 subgroup. Subgroups of order 8 in E_+(m) that are
    # nonabelian and isomorphic to D_8.
    maxsub := List(ConjugacyClassesSubgroups(G), Representative);
    candidates := Filtered(maxsub, S -> Order(S) = 8
                                    and not IsAbelian(S)
                                    and IdGroup(S) = [8, 3]);
    if Length(candidates) = 0 then
        Error("D8Section_E_plus: no D_8 section found in E_+(", m, ")");
    fi;
    H := candidates[1];
    return rec( parent := G,
                subgroup := H,
                m := m,
                order := Order(H),
                note := "D_8 = E_+(1) section" );
end;;

# ---- Q_8 section -------------------------------------------------------
#
# A Q_8 = E_-(1) section of E_+(m) exists for m >= 2 (E_+(2) has 2
# conjugacy classes of Q_8 subgroups; E_+(3) has 56; counts grow with
# m). For m = 1, E_+(1) = D_8 contains no Q_8, so the function errors.
#
# Why Q_8 sections matter (#154 / #153): the cyclic summand A(4) of
# H^3(E_+(m); U(1)) has order Z/4 at m >= 3 (Z/8 at m = 2). Restriction
# to D_8 sections kills the order-2 sub-element 2 * A(4)_gen (the
# Harada-Kono transfer-restriction obstruction). Q_8 has H^3(Q_8; U(1))
# = Z/8, is NOT a Q-isotropic preimage, and empirically detects
# 2 * A(4)_gen faithfully. So {E, D_8, Q_8} is the AM04-style detecting
# family on which Z(Vec_G^omega) -triviality can be read off.
#
# Convention. We pick the FIRST Q_8 conjugacy class representative
# returned by ConjugacyClassesSubgroups, mirroring `D8Section_E_plus`'s
# canonical choice. Multiple non-conjugate Q_8 sections exist for m
# >= 2; under the AM04 detecting-family injectivity claim (issue #193),
# any single representative gives the same triviality verdict on
# H^3(G; U(1)).
#
# Mirrors the structure of `D8Section_E_plus`. Q_8 is `SmallGroup(8,4)`
# in GAP's catalogue, so the IdGroup filter is `[8, 4]`.
Q8Section_E_plus := function(m)
    local G, maxsub, candidates, H, gens_G, H_eplus2, eplus2_subs,
          q8_in_eplus2;
    G := EPlusGroup(m);
    if m = 1 then
        Error("Q8Section_E_plus: E_+(1) = D_8 contains no Q_8 ",
              "subgroup; Q_8 sections only exist for m >= 2");
    fi;
    # For m <= 3, the full enumeration via ConjugacyClassesSubgroups is
    # fast (|G| <= 128). For m >= 4 (|G| >= 512), it becomes prohibitive;
    # instead, locate a Q_8 inside the E_+(2) subgroup generated by the
    # first 4 non-central PC generators (which form a copy of E_+(2) of
    # order 32). E_+(2) has multiple Q_8 conjugacy classes (#154); we
    # pick the first one returned by GAP's enumeration. Under AM04
    # detecting-family injectivity (#193) any single representative is
    # equivalent for the triviality verdict.
    if m >= 4 then
        gens_G := GeneratorsOfGroup(G);
        H_eplus2 := Subgroup(G, [gens_G[1], gens_G[2],
                                   gens_G[3], gens_G[4]]);
        if Order(H_eplus2) <> 32 then
            Error("Q8Section_E_plus: direct E_+(2) construction at ",
                  "m = ", m, " gave order ", Order(H_eplus2),
                  " (expected 32). PC-generator convention changed?");
        fi;
        eplus2_subs := List(ConjugacyClassesSubgroups(H_eplus2),
                             Representative);
        q8_in_eplus2 := Filtered(eplus2_subs,
            S -> Order(S) = 8 and not IsAbelian(S)
                 and IdGroup(S) = [8, 4]);
        if Length(q8_in_eplus2) = 0 then
            Error("Q8Section_E_plus: no Q_8 inside the E_+(2) subgroup ",
                  "of E_+(", m, "). PC-generator convention changed?");
        fi;
        H := q8_in_eplus2[1];
        return rec( parent := G,
                    subgroup := H,
                    m := m,
                    order := Order(H),
                    note := Concatenation(
                        "Q_8 = E_-(1) section (via E_+(2) ",
                        "subgroup, m >= 4)") );
    fi;
    # m in {2, 3}: full enumeration path.
    # Find a Q_8 subgroup. Subgroups of order 8 in E_+(m) that are
    # nonabelian and isomorphic to Q_8 (SmallGroup(8, 4)).
    maxsub := List(ConjugacyClassesSubgroups(G), Representative);
    candidates := Filtered(maxsub, S -> Order(S) = 8
                                    and not IsAbelian(S)
                                    and IdGroup(S) = [8, 4]);
    if Length(candidates) = 0 then
        Error("Q8Section_E_plus: no Q_8 section found in E_+(", m, ")");
    fi;
    H := candidates[1];
    return rec( parent := G,
                subgroup := H,
                m := m,
                order := Order(H),
                note := "Q_8 = E_-(1) section" );
end;;

# ---- H^3 of the detecting subgroup ------------------------------------
#
# Compute H^3(E; Z) where E is the maximal elementary abelian rank-(m+1)
# subgroup. We use HAP's `GroupCohomology(E, 3)` which returns the
# torsion as a list of invariant factors. By the standard
# computation of the integral cohomology of (Z/2)^k, every invariant
# factor is 2 (i.e. H^3 is elementary abelian of rank
# binomial(k+2, 3) - k = (k^3 + 2k) / 3 hmm — we just defer to HAP).
#
# For (Z/2)^{m+1}, dim_{F_2} H^3((Z/2)^{m+1}; F_2) = C(m+3, 3) but the
# integral H^3 has different dimension. HAP's GroupCohomology gives
# the integer-torsion direct-sum decomposition.

H3DetectingSubgroupTorsion := function(m)
    local ea_rec, H, torsion;
    ea_rec := MaximalElementaryAbelian_E_plus(m);
    H := ea_rec.subgroup;
    # HAP: GroupCohomology(H, 3) returns a list of integers; the
    # cohomology group is the direct sum of Z/n_i.
    torsion := GroupCohomology(H, 3);
    return rec( m := m,
                detecting_subgroup_rank := ea_rec.rank,
                detecting_subgroup_order := ea_rec.order,
                torsion := torsion );
end;;

# ---- The public API: UnitCircleCohomologyDetecting --------------------
#
# UnitCircleCohomologyDetecting(m) -- returns the H^3(H; U(1)) torsion
# vector for the maximal elementary abelian detecting subgroup
# H ≤ E_+(m). For m in {2, 3, ..., 7} this is HAP-tractable since
# |H| = 2^{m+1} stays small (256 = 2^8 for m = 7).
#
# Acceptance criteria (issue #9):
#   * m = 2, 3: agrees with `GroupCohomology(EPlusGroup(m), 3)` if
#     restricted to the 2-torsion part. (The maximal elementary
#     abelian only detects 2-torsion; higher torsion needs the D_8
#     section.)
#   * m = 7: produces a torsion vector and basis, even though
#     `GroupCohomology(EPlusGroup(7), 3)` would be infeasible.
#
# The returned record:
#   .m                      -- the m for E_+(m)
#   .group_id               -- the SmallGroup id of E_+(m) if available
#   .detecting_subgroup     -- the actual GAP subgroup H
#   .detecting_torsion      -- H^3(H; Z) torsion (list)
#   .upper_bound_note       -- string documenting that this is an
#                              upper bound for the 2-torsion part of
#                              H^3(G; U(1)).
#   .injectivity_reference  -- string citing [AM04, V.6].

UnitCircleCohomologyDetecting := function(m)
    local G, ea_rec, H_ea, torsion, group_id;
    if m < 1 then
        Error("UnitCircleCohomologyDetecting: m must be >= 1; got ", m);
    fi;
    G := EPlusGroup(m);
    ea_rec := MaximalElementaryAbelian_E_plus(m);
    H_ea := ea_rec.subgroup;
    torsion := GroupCohomology(H_ea, 3);
    if Order(G) <= 256 then
        group_id := IdGroup(G);
    else
        group_id := fail;
    fi;
    return rec( m                     := m,
                group_id              := group_id,
                group_order           := Order(G),
                detecting_subgroup    := H_ea,
                detecting_subgroup_rank   := ea_rec.rank,
                detecting_subgroup_order  := ea_rec.order,
                detecting_torsion     := torsion,
                upper_bound_note      :=
"H^3(H; U(1)) of a SINGLE rank-(m+1) elementary abelian H detects \
only PART of H^*(G; U(1)). It is NOT a full upper bound on H^3(G; U(1)) \
on its own (E_+(2) has 10 invariant factors but the rank-3 elementary \
abelian's H^3 has only 7). A genuine upper bound requires the full \
*detecting family* (E ∪ D_8 section) — see UnitCircleCohomologyDetectingFull. \
Higher torsion (Z/4, Z/8) is captured by the D_8 section, where the \
Bockstein gives Z/8.",
                injectivity_reference :=
"[Adem-Milgram, Cohomology of Finite Groups, 2nd ed., Springer 2004, \
Theorem V.6.5]: for any finite group G, the restriction map \
H^*(G; M) -> ⊕_{H ∈ F} H^*(H; M) is injective where F runs over the \
collection of elementary abelian p-subgroups detecting the cohomology. \
For E_+(m), a maximal elementary abelian (rank m+1) together with one \
D_8 = E_+(1) section forms such a detecting collection." );
end;;

# ---- Public API: UnitCircleCohomologyDetectingFull --------------------
#
# A variant that uses BOTH the maximal elementary abelian AND the
# D_8 section. The "full" output records both torsions; the actual
# H^3(G; U(1)) injects into the direct sum of these two.
#
# Returns:
#   rec( .m, .group_id, .detecting_max_ea_torsion,
#        .detecting_d8_torsion, .upper_bound_torsion, .note )
#
# Note: this is NOT a fully working "compute H^3 from the detecting
# family by inverting restriction" — that would require computing the
# image of res in H^3(H_1; U(1)) ⊕ H^3(H_2; U(1)), which requires the
# explicit restriction maps. We expose the two torsion vectors so the
# caller can reason about upper bounds.

UnitCircleCohomologyDetectingFull := function(m)
    local G, ea_rec, d8_rec, H_ea, H_d8, t_ea, t_d8, group_id, t_combined;
    if m < 1 then
        Error("UnitCircleCohomologyDetectingFull: m must be >= 1; got ", m);
    fi;
    G := EPlusGroup(m);
    ea_rec := MaximalElementaryAbelian_E_plus(m);
    d8_rec := D8Section_E_plus(m);
    H_ea := ea_rec.subgroup;
    H_d8 := d8_rec.subgroup;
    t_ea := GroupCohomology(H_ea, 3);
    t_d8 := GroupCohomology(H_d8, 3);
    if Order(G) <= 256 then
        group_id := IdGroup(G);
    else
        group_id := fail;
    fi;
    # The "combined" upper bound: H^3(G; U(1)) injects into
    # H^3(H_ea; U(1)) ⊕ H^3(H_d8; U(1)) ≅ direct sum of t_ea and t_d8.
    # As a list, concatenate.
    t_combined := Concatenation(t_ea, t_d8);
    return rec( m                          := m,
                group_id                   := group_id,
                group_order                := Order(G),
                detecting_max_ea           := H_ea,
                detecting_max_ea_rank      := ea_rec.rank,
                detecting_max_ea_torsion   := t_ea,
                detecting_d8               := H_d8,
                detecting_d8_order         := d8_rec.order,
                detecting_d8_torsion       := t_d8,
                upper_bound_torsion        := t_combined,
                note                       :=
"The combined upper bound (direct sum of torsions) bounds |H^3(E_+(m); U(1))| \
from above. The actual H^3 sits inside this sum as the image of the diagonal \
restriction. [AM04, V.6] guarantees the restriction is injective." );
end;;

# ---- Direct HAP cross-check (for m = 2, 3) ----------------------------
#
# Compute H^3(E_+(m); Z) directly via HAP for tractable m. Used by
# the test harness to validate the detecting-subgroup answer for
# small m. Returns the torsion vector.
#
# NOTE on conventions. HAP's `GroupCohomology(G, n)` returns the
# torsion of H^n(G; Z) using HAP's resolution-truncation length. For
# small groups (E_+(1) = D_8) this is robust. For E_+(2) =
# SmallGroup(32, 49) it returns [2, 2, 2, 2, 2] (the 2-rank of
# H^3(E_+(2); F_2) under HAP's "mod-2 fast" resolution), NOT the full
# integral [2,2,2,2,2,2,2,2,2,8] that one gets from
# `UniversalCoefficientsTheorem` on `TensorWithIntegers(R)` (the
# repo's canonical anchor — see spike_2divisibility.out).
#
# So for the U(1) anchor we recommend `H3UnitCircleViaUCT` below
# instead of `GroupCohomology(G, 3)`.

H3EPlusDirect := function(m)
    local G;
    G := EPlusGroup(m);
    return GroupCohomology(G, 3);
end;;

# ---- UCT-based U(1) cohomology anchor (m = 2, 3) ----------------------
#
# Builds R = ResolutionFiniteGroup(G, 4); K = TensorWithIntegers(R);
# UCT = UniversalCoefficientsTheorem(K, 3) and returns UCT.torsion,
# which is the invariant-factor decomposition of H^3(G; U(1)) = H^3(G; Z)
# torsion under the convention used throughout this codebase
# (see `transgression_kernel.g :: SetupCohomology3`).
#
# For G = E_+(2) = SmallGroup(32, 49), this returns
#   [2, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2, 8]
# matching the repo-wide anchor.

H3UnitCircleViaUCT := function(G)
    local R, K, UCT;
    R := ResolutionFiniteGroup(G, 4);
    K := TensorWithIntegers(R);
    UCT := UniversalCoefficientsTheorem(K, 3);
    return UCT.torsion;
end;;

# Convenience: anchor torsion for E_+(m) via UCT, for m = 2, 3.
H3EPlusAnchorViaUCT := function(m)
    return H3UnitCircleViaUCT(EPlusGroup(m));
end;;

# ---- Loaded notice ----------------------------------------------------

Print("Loaded quillen_detecting_subgroup.g (Q-9: detecting-subgroup path for H^3(E_+(m); U(1))).\n");
